<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Mir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1342</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Shams-ud-Din Shah Mir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Mir was the founder of the Shah Mir dynasty and of the Kashmir Sultanate. An immigrant who rose to high office at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> after the Mongol raid of 1320, he seized power from Queen Kota Rani in 1339 and ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Shams-ud-Din until 1342. His dynasty, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread in the valley, ruled Kashmir until the Chak dynasty displaced it in the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-mir.jpg" alt="Shah Mir" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Sultanates._Kashmir._AR_Sasnu_(6.26_g)._Kashmir_mint._Fixed_date_AH_842_on_reverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
